# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
from cb import *
V = R["valid"]; CV = R["conv"]
H1("9. Limitations")
P("Several limitations must be stated clearly. The first is the physical scale. With the nondimensionalisation of the base problem, the unit of length is " + g(1 / (CONST["eta"] * CONST["v"]) * 1e9, 3) + " nm and the unit of time " + g(1 / (CONST["eta"] * CONST["v"] ** 2) * 1e12, 3) + " ps for copper, so that the observation radius $r=0.3$ is about 8 nm and the window $t\\le0.3$ is about 2 ps. At such scales the continuum assumptions of the theory, the validity of a lag model for the electron–phonon exchange and the size of a realistic source are all in question. "
  "The numbers are valid for the dimensionless problem; they are not predictions for a bulk copper component, and a use of the method at larger scales requires another nondimensionalisation, which the programs allow through the property ratios, but which was not exercised.")
P("The second limitation concerns the property models. The contrast phase is hypothetical, the rule of mixtures is simple, and the porosity laws are generic power laws with assumed exponents, so that the quantitative results of Sections 8.3 and 8.4 illustrate trends and are not material data. The relaxation times $\\tau_0$ and $\\tau_1$ are kept uniform and equal in all phases, and the thermal expansion of the porous medium is unchanged by the voids. "
  "A closed-form validation exists only for uniform media. The graded case was validated by consistency, that is by the independent time integration, the mesh refinement and the energy balance, but not by an independent exact solution or by experiment. The comparison with the earlier Galerkin finite element study of a graded hollow disk [5] is qualitative only, since that problem has different geometry, boundary conditions and data, and no numbers from it are used here.")
P("The third limitation concerns the numerics. The scheme does not damp numerical oscillations at the thermal front, the peak of the stress at the front is under-resolved by about a factor of two with the baseline mesh, and the stress errors in strongly contrasting media can reach 11 per cent in the hoop stress (Table 5). The stress at the front is evaluated from the derivatives of the primary unknowns without recovery, and a stress-recovery procedure or adaptive refinement at the front would improve it. "
  "The truncation of the domain uses a reflecting Dirichlet condition and limits the computation to times before the front reaches the boundary, so that the method as implemented does not describe the long-time response of the unbounded medium. Finally, the time marching uses a constant step and a one-time factorisation, which would have to be reconsidered for nonlinear properties that depend on temperature.")
H1("10. Conclusions")
P("A Galerkin finite element method was developed and verified for the line-source problem of the modified Green–Lindsay theory in an unbounded radially graded or porous medium. The main conclusions follow.")
P("First, eliminating the operators $(1+\\tau_1\\partial_t)$ and $(1+\\tau_0\\partial_t)$ by two auxiliary state variables yields a first-order system that is marched by the trapezoidal rule with one banded factorisation. The method contains the Newmark average-acceleration method, the Crank–Nicolson method, and the Lord–Shulman-type and classical limits as special cases, and it was validated against an independent Laplace-domain finite element route to better than 0.06 per cent. "
  "Second, for uniform media, including a uniform porous medium and a medium with strongly different properties, an exact solution with arbitrary property ratios was derived and used for verification. The finite element temperature history agrees with it to " + g(V["H0"]["hist"][0], 2) + " per cent in the reference medium and to " + g(V["P_0.2"]["hist"][0], 2) + " per cent in the porous medium, the displacement to about 0.6 per cent, and the stresses to 0.1 to 0.4 per cent away from the front; near the front they differ by 1 to 4 per cent, and the peak at the front is under-resolved.")
P("Third, the regularisation of the line source by a disc of radius $a$ is the dominant error at the baseline, with the displacement error proportional to $a^2$, and it can be reduced by the choice of $a$ at the price of a finer mesh. Fourth, the global heat balance holds to better than " + sc(max(v["energy_err"] for k, v in CS.items() if not k.startswith("H0_R")), 1) + " for all graded and porous media, and the domain truncation was shown to have no effect while the front is inside the domain.")
P("Fifth, the physical results are as follows. A graded core with low conductivity delays the thermal front (arrival at $r=0.3$ moves from " + fx(arrival("H0"), 4) + " to " + fx(arrival("FG_n1"), 3) + " for $n=1$), retains heat near the source, reduces the temperature at $r=0.8$ by a factor of " + fx(at("H0", 1, 0, 0.3) / at("FG_n1", 1, 0, 0.3), 1) + " and raises the peak radial stress at $r=0.3$ by the factor " + fx(abs(peak_abs("FG_n1", 0, 2)[0] / peak_abs("H0", 0, 2)[0]), 2) + ". The response saturates for grading exponents above about 2. Porosity increases the temperature and the displacement and lowers the stresses and the wave speed. "
  "The relaxation time $\\tau_1$ does not change the temperature but removes the elastic wavefront and its stress peak, and neglecting it would overpredict the peak radial stress in the graded porous case by about " + fx(100 * (abs(peak_abs("FGP_LS", 0, 2)[0] / peak_abs("FGP_n1_0.2", 0, 2)[0]) - 1), 0) + " per cent. The relaxation time $\\tau_0$ controls the front arrival and the far-field temperature.")
P("Future work should treat radial grading of the relaxation times, temperature-dependent properties, non-reflecting boundaries for long times, stress recovery at the front, an experimental or independent numerical benchmark for a graded medium, and the extension to the plane or three-dimensional geometries with a moving source. The reference list should also be completed, as explained in the note below.")

